%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: printkit.ps — woodcut, linocut, and engraving mark presets
%%Creator: pscat — issue #52
%%EndComments
% @requires: (lib/artkit.ps) run (lib/hatchkit.ps) run (lib/surfacekit.ps) run

% A tenth sibling library: three printmaking presets — `woodcut`,
% `linocut`, `engraving` — composed entirely from `lib/hatchkit.ps`'s
% `hatch` (issue #49), `lib/artkit.ps`'s `scatter` (issue #48), and,
% optionally, `lib/surfacekit.ps`'s `grain` (issue #51). This file
% draws no geometry of its own beyond a small filled-quad "chip" mark;
% every stroke and speck a caller actually sees comes from one of
% those three, differently parameterized.
%
%   <path> opts woodcut    -   directional gouges + grain breakup
%   <path> opts linocut    -   bolder carved regions, simplified marks
%   <path> opts engraving  -   fine parallel/cross-hatched line work
%
% **API note — this file departs from surfacekit/stipplekit/
% halftonekit's `region opts NAME` convention on purpose.** Those
% presets are pure `scatter` wrappers, which only ever needs a
% `screct`/`scpath` region for its own approximate `scin` containment
% test. A printmaking preset also needs `hatch`, which needs a real
% graphics-state *clip* for an exact boundary — and a stored region's
% `/Edges` is a flattened line-segment soup with no subpath boundaries
% recorded, so rebuilding an exact `clip`-able path back out of it
% would mean re-deriving subpath breaks by coordinate-equality
% matching between adjacent edges: real machinery, for no benefit over
% just clipping to the path the caller already has current. So instead
% every preset here takes the *current path* directly, the same way
% `hatch` itself does, and manages its own `clip` internally — no
% region-building step, no pre-`clip` call, required of the caller at
% all:
%
%   newpath 40 40 moveto 260 40 lineto 260 260 lineto 40 260 lineto
%     closepath
%   << /Seed 7 >> woodcut
%
% The one discoverability risk this creates: `region opts woodcut` —
% the sibling-habit call, passing a `screct`/`scpath` result instead
% of a path — type-checks fine (a region is a dict, `woodcut`'s own
% opts argument is also a dict) but leaves the region dict sitting
% unconsumed as this operator's *first* argument, which becomes a
% stray value on the operand stack once `woodcut` pops just its own
% `opts`. `--lint`'s operand-leak check (and every test in this file's
% own suite asserting an empty operand stack after each call) is the
% backstop; this header and every `@example` below use the path form
% so the sibling-shaped call is never the one shown.
%
% **Sequencing, all three presets, identical:** `pathbbox` is read
% from the caller's real path *before* anything else runs, and handed
% to every `hatch` call as an explicit `/BBox` — never left to
% `hatch`'s own `pathbbox` default, because `scpath` (next) flattens
% the current path for the rest of this call, and a `hatch` call after
% that would read the flattened shape's own bbox instead of the
% caller's original (usually the same box, but not guaranteed for a
% curved path). Then, inside one `gsave`/`grestore`: `clip` against
% the caller's real path first (exact against true curves, not a
% flattened approximation), *then* `scpath` unconditionally captures a
% `scatter`-shaped region from that same (now-clipped) path — every
% preset calls it, even `engraving` with `/Paper false`, purely for
% uniformity (see below), not because every preset's own marks need
% the region. **The flattening is entirely local to this call,
% though, in every case, not a caller-visible cost at all:** `path` is
% part of this interpreter's saved graphics state, so the closing
% `grestore` restores the caller's path exactly as it was at entry —
% real curves included, `scpath`'s in-call flattening undone along
% with the `clip` — the same reason a caller-supplied `/Mark` override
% inside a `scatter` call can't leak state past its own `gsave`
% either. Calling `scpath` unconditionally, even where a preset's own
% marks never end up using the region, keeps every preset's behavior
% uniform rather than making `engraving` a special case that happens
% to skip a call with no caller-visible effect anyway. Every preset
% then reads `/Color`/`/Budget`/etc. from the shared options dict this
% file's header documents and *nothing else* — an unrecognized key
% (`/Spacing`, `/Count`, `/Width`, a sibling's own option name) is
% silently ignored, not forwarded, since each `hatch`/`scatter`/
% `grain` call below builds its own options dict from scratch rather
% than layering onto the caller's. One side effect worth knowing, not
% a bug: because chip marks and the optional paper pass are scattered
% *inside* the same `clip` this file already established from the
% exact path, their paint is exactly bounded by it too — better than
% `scin`'s own approximate overhang, which is all a bare `scatter`/
% `grain` call gets on its own.
%
% Shared options, all optional, identical across all three presets —
% and, deliberately, **not** the same vocabulary `scatter`/`grain`
% use for same-named keys, even though this file calls both under the
% hood: `/Scale` here is a single positive number (a size multiplier),
% not `scatter`'s `[lo hi]` range; `/Density` here is a 0..1 ink-
% coverage fraction, not `grain`'s marks-per-square-unit; `/Angle`
% here is a dominant-direction *offset*, not `hatch`'s literal sweep
% angle. A caller reaching for a sibling-shaped value out of habit
% (`/Scale [0.4 1.4]`, say) is rejected under this file's own error
% names below — validated before any derived number ever reaches
% `hatch`/`scatter`/`grain` — rather than silently misbehaving or
% surfacing one of *their* error names for a value this file, not
% they, rejected.
%
%   /Scale      num > 0 (default 1). Overall size: spacing, stroke
%               width, and chip-mark size all grow together.
%   /Density    num in [0,1] (default 0.5). Ink coverage — higher
%               tightens hatch spacing and raises chip-mark count.
%   /Roughness  num in [0,1] (default 0.5). Hand-cut irregularity —
%               higher raises hatch `/Wobble`/`/Dropout`/`/Trim` and
%               chip-mark rotation jitter. `engraving` responds to it
%               only lightly (an engraver's line is controlled by
%               design); `woodcut` responds to it most.
%   /Seed       n, `abs(n) <= 2147483645` (validated —
%               `printkit-seed-must-be-a-number`/`printkit-seed-out-
%               of-range` otherwise). Derives one sub-seed per
%               underlying call (`Seed`, `Seed+1`, `Seed+2`, ...)
%               rather than this file managing one shared `srand`/
%               `rrand` pair itself — each of `hatch`/`scatter`/
%               `grain` already seeds and restores its own stream per
%               its own documented contract, so layering a second one
%               here would just be redundant bookkeeping. The range
%               leaves headroom for the largest offset this file adds
%               (2, for `woodcut`/`linocut`'s three sub-calls) to stay
%               inside `scatter`'s own documented `+/-2147483647`
%               bound — checked here so a caller sees this file's own
%               error name rather than a raw `typecheck` from the
%               offset addition or a different file's error name from
%               a downstream call. Omit `/Seed` to let every sub-call
%               draw from the ambient stream instead, same "still
%               deterministic under the caller's own `N srand`, just
%               not independent of what drew before it" caveat those
%               files already state.
%   /Color      [r g b], each in [0,1] (default a preset-specific
%               near-black). Flat ink color — every mark this preset
%               draws (hatch strokes and chip marks alike) uses it
%               unmodulated; unlike `surfacekit`'s presets, nothing
%               here shades per-mark, since a print's ink is flat.
%   /Budget     num > 0, <= 200000 (default 20000 — `hatch`'s and
%               `scatter`'s own default, so this file never *lowers*
%               either primitive's own ceiling unless a caller asks
%               it to). Forwarded as `hatch`'s `/MaxLines` to every
%               hatch call this preset makes (for `engraving`, one
%               combined multi-angle call — `/MaxLines` there already
%               bounds the *summed* candidate count across all three
%               angles, hatchkit's own documented contract, not
%               `/Budget` per angle) and as `scatter`'s `/Budget` to
%               the chip-mark pass and the optional paper pass. At
%               most three such sub-calls ever run (one hatch pass,
%               one chip scatter, one paper `grain`), each
%               independently capped at `/Budget` — so total work is
%               bounded by a small constant multiple of `/Budget`,
%               never unbounded, regardless of region size or option
%               values.
%   /Paper      bool (default false). When true, an extra
%               `surfacekit` `grain` pass (issue #51) runs first,
%               under the ink, at low `/Strength` — closing the
%               "interplay with surfacekit" this file was left to
%               decide (NOTES.md's halftonekit entry). Like every
%               `surfacekit` preset, `grain` lerps toward paper-white,
%               not toward transparent (no portable PostScript alpha
%               operator — the same Ghostscript-portability constraint
%               `surfacekit`'s own header documents), so `/Paper true`
%               assumes a light page underneath.
%   /Angle      num, degrees (default 0). `woodcut`/`linocut`'s single
%               dominant grain direction; `engraving`'s three fixed
%               angles (60 degrees apart) are offset by this value
%               together, not independently settable — a caller
%               wanting different engraving angles calls `hatch`
%               directly instead.
%
% **Reproducibility**, same scope every other `rand`-driven procedure
% in this codebase states: a given path, options, and `/Seed` draw the
% same picture in *this* interpreter every time. Ghostscript uses a
% different generator, so the same seeded call there renders an
% equally deterministic, equally reproducible, but different
% arrangement — "runs unchanged in pscat and Ghostscript" means runs
% without error and stays visually in-genre, not pixel-identical
% between them; the `ghostscript_accepts_*` test in this file's own
% suite is what actually stands behind that claim, exactly as
% `hatchkit.ps`'s own header already frames it.
%
% Scratch prefix: every internal name starts with `pr` (`printkit`) —
% not `pk` (already `paintkit.ps`'s), not `p*` alone (too short to be
% collision-safe against this codebase's many single-letter-prefixed
% files). Grepped clean against every `lib/*.ps`/`lib/styles/*.ps`
% file's own `/pr...` names (not just top-level `def`s — halftonekit's
% own header notes that a top-level-only grep is exactly what missed
% a real collision there): the only hits are `graph.ps`'s public
% `/proc`/`/project3`, unrelated public names in a file `printkit.ps`
% never loads alongside in a namespace-sensitive way, not `pr`-
% prefixed scratch state.

% --- shared validation -------------------------------------------------

% @internal
/prisnum { type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or } def

% dict key default prgetdef -> value
% printkit's own copy of the wrap-the-default-in-an-array trick every
% sibling in this codebase repeats under its own scratch prefix (see
% hatchkit's `hkgetdef`/surfacekit's `sfgetdef` for the same reasoning
% stated once each).
% @internal
/prgetdef {
  /prgdd exch 1 array astore def
  /prgdk exch def
  /prgdt exch def
  prgdt prgdk known { prgdt prgdk get } { prgdd 0 get } ifelse
} def

% val errproc name prbindnum -
% printkit's own copy of surfacekit's `sfbindnum`: validates `val` is
% a number (else runs `errproc`, a manifest `{ self-documenting-name }`
% literal at every call site), then binds it to `name`. `val` stays
% inside a private 1-element array until the check passes, so a
% caller-supplied procedure standing in for a numeric option can never
% auto-execute on a bare reference before its type is checked.
% @internal
/prbindnum {
  /prbnname exch def
  /prbnerr exch def
  1 array astore /prbnbox exch def
  prbnbox 0 get prisnum not { prbnerr exec } if
  prbnname prbnbox 0 get def
} def

% opts default3arr prcolordef -> r g b
% printkit's own copy of surfacekit's `sfcolordef`: resolves and
% validates `/Color` (or `default3arr`), a 3-element array of numbers
% each in [0,1], entirely inside private 1-element-array boxes until
% every check passes — the same auto-exec hazard `sfcolordef`'s own
% comment documents, since `type /arraytype eq` alone does not rule
% out a procedure (a procedure *is* an executable array).
% @internal
/prcolordef {
  /prcddef exch def
  /prcdopts exch def
  prcdopts /Color prcddef prgetdef 1 array astore /prcdbox exch def
  prcdbox 0 get type /arraytype ne { printkit-color-must-be-an-rgb-array } if
  prcdbox 0 get length 3 ne { printkit-color-must-be-an-rgb-array } if
  prcdbox 0 get 0 get prisnum prcdbox 0 get 1 get prisnum and
    prcdbox 0 get 2 get prisnum and not
    { printkit-color-must-be-an-rgb-array } if
  prcdbox 0 get 0 get dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or
  prcdbox 0 get 1 get dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or or
  prcdbox 0 get 2 get dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or or
    { printkit-color-components-must-be-0-to-1 } if
  prcdbox 0 get 0 get prcdbox 0 get 1 get prcdbox 0 get 2 get
} def

% opts default prboolget -> bool
% @internal
/prboolget {
  /prbgdef exch def
  /prbgopts exch def
  prbgopts /Paper prbgdef prgetdef 1 array astore /prbgbox exch def
  prbgbox 0 get type /booleantype ne { printkit-paper-must-be-a-boolean } if
  prbgbox 0 get
} def

% dict offset resolved prapplyseed -
% If `resolved` (a `propts` result) carries `/Seed`, puts
% `(Seed + offset)` into `dict` at `/Seed` — the per-sub-call seed
% derivation this file's header describes. A no-op otherwise, so the
% sub-call draws from the ambient stream exactly as it would with no
% `/Seed` key at all.
% @internal
/prapplyseed {
  /prasres exch def
  /prasoff exch def
  /prasdict exch def
  prasres /Seed known {
    prasdict /Seed prasres /Seed get prasoff add put
  } if
} def

% defaultcolor3arr opts errproc propts -> resolved
% Validates and resolves every shared option this file's header
% documents into a fresh dict: `/Scale` `/Density` `/Roughness`
% `/Angle` `/Budget` `/Paper` `/ColorR` `/ColorG` `/ColorB`, plus
% `/Seed` when the caller supplied one. `errproc` (a manifest `{...}`
% literal at every call site, same self-documenting-error convention
% `sfregiondef`/`sfbindnum` use) is each preset's own
% `<preset>-opts-must-be-a-dict` name — matching the sibling
% convention of a per-operator name for that one check — while every
% other error below is `printkit`-owned, shared across all three
% presets since the options themselves are one shared vocabulary.
% Every check here runs before any derived value reaches
% `hatch`/`scatter`/`grain` — see this file's header for why that
% matters given the deliberate vocabulary overlap with those files'
% own same-named options.
% @internal
/propts {
  /proerr exch def
  /prodef exch def
  1 array astore /probox exch def
  probox 0 get type /dicttype ne { proerr exec } if
  /proopts probox 0 get def

  proopts /Scale 1 prgetdef
    { printkit-scale-must-be-a-number } /proscale prbindnum
  proscale 0 le { printkit-scale-must-be-positive } if

  proopts /Density 0.5 prgetdef
    { printkit-density-must-be-a-number } /prodensity prbindnum
  prodensity 0 lt prodensity 1 gt or
    { printkit-density-must-be-a-fraction-in-0-1 } if

  proopts /Roughness 0.5 prgetdef
    { printkit-roughness-must-be-a-number } /proroughness prbindnum
  proroughness 0 lt proroughness 1 gt or
    { printkit-roughness-must-be-a-fraction-in-0-1 } if

  proopts /Angle 0 prgetdef
    { printkit-angle-must-be-a-number } /proangle prbindnum

  proopts /Budget 20000 prgetdef
    { printkit-budget-must-be-a-number } /probudget prbindnum
  probudget 0 le { printkit-budget-must-be-positive } if
  probudget 200000 gt { printkit-budget-must-be-200000-or-fewer } if

  proopts false prboolget /propaper exch def

  proopts prodef prcolordef
    /procolb exch def /procolg exch def /procolr exch def

  % /Seed is optional (prores simply omits it when the caller never
  % gave one, and prapplyseed no-ops on a missing key) but, when
  % given, must be validated here too: `prapplyseed` derives each
  % sub-call's own seed as `Seed + offset` (offset up to 2, for
  % woodcut/linocut's three sub-calls), and without a check here a
  % non-numeric `/Seed` fails with a raw `typecheck` from `add` deep
  % inside `prapplyseed`, and a `/Seed` near `scatter`'s own
  % documented +/-2147483647 bound fails with *its* name instead of
  % this file's own -- inconsistently between presets, since they add
  % different maximum offsets (Codex review, PR #128).
  proopts /Seed known {
    proopts /Seed get { printkit-seed-must-be-a-number } /proseed prbindnum
    proseed abs 2147483645 gt { printkit-seed-out-of-range } if
  } if

  /prores 10 dict def
  prores /Scale proscale put
  prores /Density prodensity put
  prores /Roughness proroughness put
  prores /Angle proangle put
  prores /Budget probudget put
  prores /Paper propaper put
  prores /ColorR procolr put
  prores /ColorG procolg put
  prores /ColorB procolb put
  proopts /Seed known { prores /Seed proseed put } if
  prores
} def

% --- chip mark (woodcut/linocut carved-texture marks) ------------------

% x y scale angle prchipmark -
% A short filled quad, centered at (x,y), rotated by `angle`, sized
% from the current `/prchiplen`/`/prchipwid` (each preset sets these
% right before its own `scatter` call — sequential, never nested, so
% no collision between `woodcut`'s and `linocut`'s own values). Draws
% in whatever color is already current; unlike `surfacekit`'s default
% marks, this file sets ink color once per preset call rather than
% per-mark, since print ink is flat, not shaded. Consumes all four
% operands per `scatter`'s own `/Mark` contract and touches no `sc-`/
% `sq-`/`si-` name.
% @internal
/prchipmark {
  /prcmang exch def
  /prcmscale exch def
  /prcmy exch def
  /prcmx exch def
  /prcmlen prchiplen prcmscale mul def
  /prcmwid prchipwid prcmscale mul def
  gsave
    prcmx prcmy translate
    prcmang rotate
    newpath
    prcmlen -2 div prcmwid -2 div moveto
    prcmlen 2 div prcmwid -2 div lineto
    prcmlen 2 div prcmwid 2 div lineto
    prcmlen -2 div prcmwid 2 div lineto
    closepath
    fill
  grestore
} def

% --- woodcut -------------------------------------------------------

% <path> opts woodcut -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Woodcut preset — one directional hatch pass (the grain), with high wobble/dropout/trim for hand-cut breakup, plus a scatter of small jittered chip marks along the grain. Built on hatchkit's hatch and artkit's scatter; clips to the current path internally, so no caller pre-clip or region object is needed.
% @example: newpath 20 20 moveto 180 20 lineto 180 180 lineto 20 180 lineto closepath << /Seed 5 >> woodcut
% @param: /Scale num > 0, overall size multiplier (default 1)
% @param: /Density num in [0,1], ink coverage (default 0.5)
% @param: /Roughness num in [0,1], hand-cut irregularity (default 0.5)
% @param: /Seed n, derives one sub-seed per underlying call (default: ambient stream)
% @param: /Color [r g b] ink color (default a warm near-black)
% @param: /Budget num, per-sub-call safety cap forwarded to hatch/scatter (default 20000)
% @param: /Paper bool, lay a subtle surfacekit grain pass under the ink (default false)
% @param: /Angle num, degrees, dominant grain direction (default 0)
/woodcut {
  [ 0.09 0.07 0.05 ] { woodcut-opts-must-be-a-dict } propts /prres exch def
  /prscale prres /Scale get def
  /prdensity prres /Density get def
  /prroughness prres /Roughness get def
  /prangle prres /Angle get def
  /prbudget prres /Budget get def

  pathbbox
  /prbby1 exch def /prbbx1 exch def /prbby0 exch def /prbbx0 exch def
  /prbbox [ prbbx0 prbby0 prbbx1 prbby1 ] def

  gsave
  clip

  /prregion scpath def

  prres /Paper get {
    /prpopts 4 dict def
    prpopts /Density 0.02 put
    prpopts /Strength 0.18 put
    prpopts /Budget prbudget put
    prpopts 0 prres prapplyseed
    prregion prpopts grain
  } if

  prres /ColorR get prres /ColorG get prres /ColorB get setrgbcolor

  /prwspacing 16 0.3 prdensity 1.4 mul add div prscale mul def
  /prwwidthlo 1.6 prscale mul def
  /prwwidthhi 3.2 prroughness 1.5 mul add prscale mul def
  /prwwobble 3.0 prroughness mul prscale mul def
  /prwdropout 0.32 prroughness mul def
  /prwtrimhi 0.4 prroughness mul def

  /prhopts 8 dict def
  prhopts /BBox prbbox put
  prhopts /Angle prangle put
  prhopts /Spacing prwspacing put
  prhopts /Width [ prwwidthlo prwwidthhi ] put
  prhopts /Wobble prwwobble put
  prhopts /Dropout prwdropout put
  prhopts /Trim [ 0 prwtrimhi ] put
  prhopts /MaxLines prbudget put
  prhopts 1 prres prapplyseed
  prhopts hatch

  /prwchipcount 140 prdensity mul 0.5 add cvi def
  /prchiplen 5 prscale mul def
  /prchipwid 1.6 prscale mul def
  /prwjitter 18 40 prroughness mul add def

  prwchipcount 0 gt {
    /prsopts 6 dict def
    prsopts /Count prwchipcount put
    prsopts /Scale [ 0.7 1.4 ] put
    prsopts /Rotate [ prangle prwjitter sub prangle prwjitter add ] put
    prsopts /Mark /prchipmark load put
    prsopts /Budget prbudget put
    prsopts 2 prres prapplyseed
    prregion prsopts scatter
  } if

  grestore
} def

% --- linocut -------------------------------------------------------

% <path> opts linocut -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Linocut preset — one bold, low-wobble hatch pass and a sparse scatter of a few large chip marks (simplified, deliberate carving, not grain-following breakup). Built on hatchkit's hatch and artkit's scatter; clips to the current path internally.
% @example: newpath 20 20 moveto 180 20 lineto 180 180 lineto 20 180 lineto closepath << /Seed 5 >> linocut
% @param: /Scale num > 0, overall size multiplier (default 1)
% @param: /Density num in [0,1], ink coverage (default 0.5)
% @param: /Roughness num in [0,1], hand-cut irregularity (default 0.5)
% @param: /Seed n, derives one sub-seed per underlying call (default: ambient stream)
% @param: /Color [r g b] ink color (default a flat near-black)
% @param: /Budget num, per-sub-call safety cap forwarded to hatch/scatter (default 20000)
% @param: /Paper bool, lay a subtle surfacekit grain pass under the ink (default false)
% @param: /Angle num, degrees, dominant carve direction (default 0)
/linocut {
  [ 0.04 0.04 0.04 ] { linocut-opts-must-be-a-dict } propts /prres exch def
  /prscale prres /Scale get def
  /prdensity prres /Density get def
  /prroughness prres /Roughness get def
  /prangle prres /Angle get def
  /prbudget prres /Budget get def

  pathbbox
  /prbby1 exch def /prbbx1 exch def /prbby0 exch def /prbbx0 exch def
  /prbbox [ prbbx0 prbby0 prbbx1 prbby1 ] def

  gsave
  clip

  /prregion scpath def

  prres /Paper get {
    /prpopts 4 dict def
    prpopts /Density 0.02 put
    prpopts /Strength 0.18 put
    prpopts /Budget prbudget put
    prpopts 0 prres prapplyseed
    prregion prpopts grain
  } if

  prres /ColorR get prres /ColorG get prres /ColorB get setrgbcolor

  /prlspacing 26 0.3 prdensity 1.4 mul add div prscale mul def
  /prlwidthlo 2.6 prscale mul def
  /prlwidthhi 4.6 prroughness 0.8 mul add prscale mul def
  /prlwobble 0.8 prroughness mul prscale mul def
  /prldropout 0.10 prroughness mul def
  /prltrimhi 0.15 prroughness mul def

  /prhopts 8 dict def
  prhopts /BBox prbbox put
  prhopts /Angle prangle put
  prhopts /Spacing prlspacing put
  prhopts /Width [ prlwidthlo prlwidthhi ] put
  prhopts /Wobble prlwobble put
  prhopts /Dropout prldropout put
  prhopts /Trim [ 0 prltrimhi ] put
  prhopts /MaxLines prbudget put
  prhopts 1 prres prapplyseed
  prhopts hatch

  /prlchipcount 18 prdensity mul 0.5 add cvi def
  /prchiplen 11 prscale mul def
  /prchipwid 4.5 prscale mul def
  /prljitter 8 10 prroughness mul add def

  prlchipcount 0 gt {
    /prsopts 6 dict def
    prsopts /Count prlchipcount put
    prsopts /Scale [ 0.85 1.25 ] put
    prsopts /Rotate [ prangle prljitter sub prangle prljitter add ] put
    prsopts /Mark /prchipmark load put
    prsopts /Budget prbudget put
    prsopts 2 prres prapplyseed
    prregion prsopts scatter
  } if

  grestore
} def

% --- engraving -------------------------------------------------------

% <path> opts engraving -
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Engraving preset — one hatchkit call sweeping three angles (60 degrees apart) at fine spacing, thin width, and minimal wobble/dropout: controlled crosshatched line work, no chip marks. Built entirely on hatchkit's hatch; clips to the current path internally.
% @example: newpath 20 20 moveto 180 20 lineto 180 180 lineto 20 180 lineto closepath << /Seed 5 >> engraving
% @param: /Scale num > 0, overall size multiplier (default 1)
% @param: /Density num in [0,1], ink coverage (default 0.5)
% @param: /Roughness num in [0,1], line irregularity (default 0.5, applied lightly)
% @param: /Seed n, derives one sub-seed per underlying call (default: ambient stream)
% @param: /Color [r g b] ink color (default a cool near-black)
% @param: /Budget num, safety cap on the combined three-angle line count (default 20000)
% @param: /Paper bool, lay a subtle surfacekit grain pass under the ink (default false)
% @param: /Angle num, degrees, offset applied to all three fixed angles (default 0)
/engraving {
  [ 0.05 0.05 0.09 ] { engraving-opts-must-be-a-dict } propts /prres exch def
  /prscale prres /Scale get def
  /prdensity prres /Density get def
  /prroughness prres /Roughness get def
  /prangle prres /Angle get def
  /prbudget prres /Budget get def

  pathbbox
  /prbby1 exch def /prbbx1 exch def /prbby0 exch def /prbbx0 exch def
  /prbbox [ prbbx0 prbby0 prbbx1 prbby1 ] def

  gsave
  clip

  /prregion scpath def

  prres /Paper get {
    /prpopts 4 dict def
    prpopts /Density 0.02 put
    prpopts /Strength 0.18 put
    prpopts /Budget prbudget put
    prpopts 0 prres prapplyseed
    prregion prpopts grain
  } if

  prres /ColorR get prres /ColorG get prres /ColorB get setrgbcolor

  /prespacing 4.5 0.3 prdensity 1.4 mul add div prscale mul def
  /prewidthlo 0.5 prscale mul def
  /prewidthhi 1.0 prscale mul def
  /prewobble 0.25 prroughness mul prscale mul def
  /predropout 0.05 prroughness mul def
  /pretrimhi 0.05 prroughness mul def

  /prhopts 8 dict def
  prhopts /BBox prbbox put
  prhopts /Angles [ prangle prangle 60 add prangle 120 add ] put
  prhopts /Spacing prespacing put
  prhopts /Width [ prewidthlo prewidthhi ] put
  prhopts /Wobble prewobble put
  prhopts /Dropout predropout put
  prhopts /Trim [ 0 pretrimhi ] put
  prhopts /MaxLines prbudget put
  prhopts 1 prres prapplyseed
  prhopts hatch

  grestore
} def
